# Citation metadata, in the Citation File Format (https://citation-file-format.org).
# GitHub reads this and shows a "Cite this repository" button; Zenodo reads it
# when minting a DOI from a release.
#
####   RELEASE CHECKLIST.  The full runbook is PUBLISHING.md; these are the
####   steps that go wrong inside this file.
####
####   1. Enable the repository in Zenodo BEFORE tagging.  Zenodo archives
####      releases made after it is switched on and does not reach back for
####      earlier ones.  This is the one ordering constraint that cannot be
####      repaired afterward.
####   2. On each tagged release, bump `version` and `date-released` to match.
####      `version` here must equal the one a run log reports, or the citation
####      names software that did not produce the result.  The test suite holds
####      it equal to MLOS_VERSION in mlos_common.R and to pyproject.toml.
####      `version` is bumped when the work is done rather than when the tag
####      lands, so `date-released` runs ahead of the tag until CHANGELOG.md's
####      Unreleased section is given its number; check it at tag time.
####   3. Check the date in UTC (`TZ=UTC date +%F`) before tagging.  GitHub
####      stamps tags in UTC and Zenodo dates records in Geneva local time, so
####      an evening tag on the United States west coast lands on the next day
####      in both of them and disagrees with what is written here.
####   4. For `doi:` below, use Zenodo's CONCEPT DOI, the version-independent
####      one that always resolves to the newest release, not the DOI of a
####      single release.  A release's own DOI does not exist until that
####      release is archived, by which point this file is already inside it,
####      so it could only ever name the release before.  The concept DOI is
####      stable, and `version` above is what says which release was actually
####      run.  There is therefore no `doi:` field until the release after the
####      first archived one.
####   5. AFTER publishing a release, edit the Zenodo record's `version` field
####      to drop the leading `v`.  Zenodo takes that field from the tag name
####      rather than from `version` below, so the record reads `v0.1.0` where
####      a run log reads `0.1.0`, and the pair a reader checks is the DOI and
####      the version.  This has needed doing on every release so far, so
####      treat it as part of publishing rather than as something to check
####      for.  It is not confined to records built from a GitHub release; a
####      deposit uploaded by hand has arrived with a `v` too, so check every
####      record.  Check it in the Zenodo interface at the time: reading a
####      record back afterward shows its current state, not what was minted.
####      The bare form is correct rather than merely preferred: semantic
####      versioning treats `v0.1.0` as a tag name and `0.1.0` as the version.
#
####   LICENSE.  `license` below names the license of the code, which is what
####   this file is metadata for.  The two guides, mlos_user_guide.md and
####   mlos_math_methods.md, are CC BY 4.0; see README.md.  Naming both here
####   would leave Zenodo to choose one for its record, so the record's own
####   description is where the documentation license belongs.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it as below.  If you use results
  computed from the Orange County data that the shipped settings files cover,
  also cite the deposit the prepared input comes from, 10.5281/zenodo.22051368,
  alongside it.  If you use the OC2 results as they are rather than recomputing
  them, cite 10.5281/zenodo.22084231 instead of that one.
title: "mLOS"
abstract: >-
  Survival analysis of length of stay in animal shelters.  A run is one YAML
  settings file and one prepared CSV of shelter stays, and produces
  Kaplan-Meier, Cox proportional-hazards, and Aalen-Johansen competing-risks
  results comparing time in care across user-defined periods, intake types, or
  animal groups.  Output is a consolidated Excel workbook, a plot set with the
  numerical values of every curve beside it, a JSON bundle holding every
  computed number, and a record of how the input rows were transformed, removed
  or kept.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
    given-names: "Michael Loizos"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
version: "0.1.2"
date-released: "2026-08-28"
license: MIT
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22083814"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Shelter-Data-Analysis/mLOS"
keywords:
  - animal shelter
  - length of stay
  - survival analysis
  - Kaplan-Meier
  - competing risks
  - reproducible research

# The chain this software sits in: the prepared files it reads, the tool that
# produced them, what it writes from them, and the raw extracts behind it all.
# The data deposits are cited by their VERSION DOI rather than their concept
# DOI, and the software by its concept DOI.  That is one rule applied twice rather than
# two rules: precision has to live somewhere, and for software it lives in the
# version number beside the DOI, while for data it lives in the digest a run
# log pins, so only a version DOI is guaranteed to still hold the bytes that
# produced a result.
#
# If a deposit's own title or author list changes, change it here too: a
# citation that does not match the record is not a citation.
references:
  - type: data
    title: >-
      Processed animal-level data, with descriptive statistics, from
      government-operated animal shelters in Orange County and Los Angeles
      County, California
    authors:
      - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
        given-names: "Michael Loizos"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
    doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22051368"
    version: "1"
    year: 2026
    license: CC-BY-4.0
    notes: >-
      The direct input.  data/OC1_data.csv and data/OC2_data.csv in this
      repository are byte-identical to two of the files in this deposit, and
      their statistics tables are the first half of the flow that
      data_preparation_stats.csv continues.  A run over your own shelter's
      data has nothing to do with this deposit and should not cite it.
  - type: software
    title: "ShelterDataPrep"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
        given-names: "Michael Loizos"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
    doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22051338"
    year: 2026
    license: MIT
    repository-code: "https://github.com/Shelter-Data-Analysis/ShelterDataPrep"
    notes: >-
      Prepares a raw shelter extract into the input format this tool reads.
      Cite it with the version a preparation run log reports; the shipped
      inputs were prepared under 0.3.0.
  - type: data
    title: >-
      mLOS results for the OC2 study: length-of-stay analysis of Orange County
      California shelter data (mLOS 0.1.0)
    authors:
      - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
        given-names: "Michael Loizos"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
    doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22084231"
    version: "1"
    year: 2026
    license: CC-BY-4.0
    notes: >-
      What this software produces from the prepared input above, under 0.1.0:
      the workbook, the plots with the numerical values of every curve beside
      them, the results bundle, the console log, and the settings file that
      produced all of it.  Cite this instead of the prepared input if you used
      the results as they are rather than recomputing them.  The slide deck
      built from this run is deposited separately, at
      10.5281/zenodo.22135419; it is not listed here because the deck is
      experimental and is not what the analysis reports.
  - type: data
    title: >-
      Raw animal-level data from government-operated animal shelters in Orange
      County and Los Angeles County, California
    authors:
      - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
        given-names: "Michael Loizos"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
    doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22051091"
    version: "1"
    year: 2026
    license: CC-BY-4.0
    notes: >-
      One step further upstream: the extract ShelterDataPrep read to produce
      the prepared files above.  Cite it when the preparation itself is part of
      what is being reported.
